{
  "id": "ep175",
  "duration": 56.057,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.961,
      "text": "Our universe is 13.8 billion years old, but 93 billion light-years wide.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Dark Flow (4423361836).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dark_Flow_(4423361836).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 5.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.961,
      "end": 12.956,
      "text": "Since nothing outruns light, that cosmic sphere should logically measure under 28 billion light-years across.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "CMB universe expansion.png",
        "credit": "NASA/WMAP Science Team/ Art by Dana Berry",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CMB_universe_expansion.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.967,
          "end": 12.967,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.956,
      "end": 22.501,
      "text": "When the oldest detectable glow was emitted 380,000 years after the Big Bang, its source sat just 42 million light-years away.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Oldest Light in the Universe (SVS30133).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA's Scientific Visualization Studio - Marit Jentoft-Nilsen, Global Science and Technology, Inc./Mark Malanoski",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oldest_Light_in_the_Universe_(SVS30133).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.967,
          "end": 16.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 16.867,
          "end": 22.5,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 22.501,
      "end": 30.371,
      "text": "Yet as those ancient photons spent 13.8 billion years journeying toward Earth, the distance multiplied between us.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-HS201427a-HubbleUltraDeepField2014-20140603.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, H. Teplitz and M. Rafelski (IPAC/Caltech), A. Koekemoer (STScI), R. Windhorst (Arizona State University), and Z. Levay (STScI)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-HS201427a-HubbleUltraDeepField2014-20140603.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 22.5,
          "end": 26.433,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 26.433,
          "end": 30.367,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 30.371,
      "end": 36.466,
      "text": "Albert Einstein proved no physical object can move locally through space faster than light.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Einstein 1921 by F Schmutzer - restoration.jpg",
        "credit": "Ferdinand Schmutzer / Adam Cuerden",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einstein_1921_by_F_Schmutzer_-_restoration.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 30.367,
          "end": 34.2,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 34.2,
          "end": 36.467,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.466,
      "end": 43.491,
      "text": "But the fabric of space itself has no speed limit, stretching relentlessly between receding galaxies.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Probing the cosmic web (opo0820b).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, and E. Hallman (University of Colorado, Boulder)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Probing_the_cosmic_web_(opo0820b).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.467,
          "end": 39.133,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 39.133,
          "end": 43.5,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.491,
      "end": 50.361,
      "text": "That ancient matter was carried 46 billion light-years away while its original glow was still in transit.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Webb's First Deep Field.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, and STScI",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Webb%27s_First_Deep_Field.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.5,
          "end": 46.0,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 46.0,
          "end": 50.367,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 50.361,
      "end": 55.707,
      "text": "Cosmic expansion simply stretched the distance far faster than photons could fly.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Dark Flow (4423361836).jpg",
        "credit": "NASA Goddard Space Flight Center from Greenbelt, MD, USA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dark_Flow_(4423361836).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 50.367,
          "end": 56.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
